NextKey API — first run against the real chain
==============================================

2026-09-10, `npm run api:dev` (wrangler 4.88.0, local runtime, Windows).

The worker had never seen a chain before this: it was written and reviewed in a
container whose proxy refuses the Sepolia RPC. So this is the run that turns
"written" into "works", and it is kept for the two things it settles rather than
for the fact that it answered at all.


1 · The Universal Resolver override took
----------------------------------------

    GET http://localhost:8787/demo/v1/health

    {
      "ok": true,
      "network": "sepolia",
      "api": "1",
      "universalResolver": "0xd26f2040d083af1cd2962ba303f4bea0c4faf142",
      "records": {
        "pubkey": "nextkey.pubkey",
        "eph": "nextkey.eph",
        "sealed": "nextkey.eph.sealed",
        "secret": "nextkey.secret"
      },
      "writes": false,
      "logs": false
    }

`0xd26f…f142` is the hackathon deployment's resolver, not the one viem ships for
Sepolia. That distinction is the whole reason this field is in the health
response: without the override every lookup quietly queries the *production*
deployment and returns null — no error, no warning, and an empty answer that
looks exactly like a name with nothing on it. This project lost a day to that
once, and the health route now states which resolver it is reading rather than
leaving it to be inferred from an empty result.

`writes: false` and `logs: false` are the other two: no route writes, and no
storage is attached for a name to be written into. Said in the answer that
reports health, because a health check that only ever says "ok" teaches a caller
nothing about the shape of what they are talking to.


2 · A real name, and an ID that two independent implementations agree on
------------------------------------------------------------------------

    GET http://localhost:8787/demo/v1/id/anna.nextkey.eth

    {
      "network": "sepolia",
      "name": "anna.nextkey.eth",
      "nextkeyId": "NK-X3B7Q-6AG76-YWZG5",
      "pubkey": "k5aC2G4AISBxAIweGzqdC+6mHGhxu3aucfwz8V0V6wA="
    }

The key was read live from `nextkey.pubkey` on `anna.nextkey.eth`. The ID was
then recomputed from that base64 value by a separate implementation of the rule,
written from the specification rather than imported from the source, and it
matched character for character:

    key bytes : 32
    computed  : NK-X3B7Q-6AG76-YWZG5
    API said  : NK-X3B7Q-6AG76-YWZG5
    agree     : true

Worth being precise about what that does and does not prove. `web/test/interop.mjs`
already checks that the browser and the command line derive the same ID, in Node
and again inside Chromium — but both of those import the same module, so they
agree by construction. This is the first check against an implementation that
does not, and against a key that came off the chain rather than out of a test.

A NextKey ID is presentation, not cryptography, which is exactly why a
disagreement would be expensive: two people comparing IDs — one reading the
Sandbox page, one reading a command line — would conclude they were talking
about different keys, and the correct response to that is to not send the
secret. A wrong "no" costs as much as a wrong "yes".


3 · The refusals, provoked rather than described
------------------------------------------------

The first version of this log said these were written, reviewed and never seen
to fire. They have now fired, and one of them exists because of what happened
when they were tried.

    GET /demo/v1/id/hero01.nextkey.eth      404 no_published_key
    GET /demo/v1/name/vitalik.eth           200 role "unregistered"
    GET /demo/v1/id/vitalik.eth             404 not_on_this_deployment
    GET /v1/health                          501 network_not_live

`hero01.nextkey.eth` and `vitalik.eth` are the pair that matters, because they
are the two answers this endpoint got wrong on its first run. `hero01` is one of
the lent pool names: registered here, resolver attached, no key published yet —
so it is `no_published_key`, a fact about what the name carries. `vitalik.eth`
is registered on production ENS and not on this deployment at all, and the first
version answered it with

    "role": "empty"

— "this name carries none of NextKey's records", which quietly asserts that the
name exists. It was a confident description of a name the endpoint had never
found.

The cause is that a text lookup answers the same empty value for a name that
carries nothing and for a name that is not there. They are separated now by
asking which resolver answers, with three outcomes rather than two: an address,
`null` for nothing resolving, and *unknown* when that lookup itself fails. The
one-line version — a try/catch returning null — would have turned every RPC
hiccup into "this name does not exist", which is the failure being fixed rather
than a smaller one.

    "role": "unregistered",
    "resolver": null,
    "resolverKnown": true,
    "note": "Nothing resolves for this name on the hackathon deployment. The
             empty records below are what the resolver was not there to answer,
             not what it answered."

And the name that does resolve now says which resolver answered, so a reader can
tell an absent record from an absent name without taking the note's word for it:

    "name": "anna.nextkey.eth",
    "role": "recipient",
    "resolver": "0x52A02f288AA5dde082206D85d4001880D64F4101",
    "receivable": true,
    "nextkeyId": "NK-X3B7Q-6AG76-YWZG5"

Worth recording plainly: this is the third time in this repository that absence
and failure-to-find have been spelled the same way. The event scanner reported
"no events found" when all forty-five of its requests had been refused. The
simulation approved a write to an address with no code, because silence is the
valid answer for a setter with no return value. Both are in this log. The
pattern is not carelessness in one place — it is that the honest answer costs an
extra round trip, and the dishonest one is always one line shorter.


What this run still does not show
---------------------------------

The deployed worker. This was `wrangler dev` on localhost against the real
Sepolia; the routes, the resolution, the ID and the four refusals are exercised,
the Cloudflare edge is not.

`502 upstream_unavailable` and `422 key_not_x25519` remain unprovoked. The first
needs a node that refuses, the second a name whose `nextkey.pubkey` holds
something that is not a 32-byte key — neither of which exists to hand. A check
nobody has seen fail is not a check, so they are not claimed as evidenced.
